---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
// The hosted pilot call for applications. Public pitches go to the
// "Hosted pilot" category in GitHub Discussions, which has a form
// (.github/DISCUSSION_TEMPLATE/hosted-pilot.yml in the main repo);
// private ones go by email with the same questions.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { DEMO_URL, REPO_URL } from '../lib/site';

const PILOT_EMAIL = 'matthew@gratisgis.org';
const DEADLINE = 'January 31, 2027';
const PUBLIC_URL = `${REPO_URL}/discussions/new?category=hosted-pilot`;
const QUESTIONS = [
  'Organization name and type (agency, school, nonprofit, company)',
  'How many people would use it',
  'What you use today (ArcGIS Online, ArcGIS Enterprise, QGIS only, paper, something else)',
  'One or two real workflows you would run on it',
  'Who would be the day-to-day contact',
  'Would you be open to a public case study at the end',
];
const mailBody = `${QUESTIONS.map((q, i) => `${i + 1}. ${q}:\n`).join('\n')}`;
const mailto = `mailto:${PILOT_EMAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('GratisGIS hosted pilot')}&body=${encodeURIComponent(mailBody)}`;
---

<Base
  title="Hosted pilot: 12 months of GratisGIS, run for you | GratisGIS"
  description="One organization gets GratisGIS hosted and looked after for 12 months at no cost, in return for real use and honest feedback. Applications close January 31, 2027."
  path="/pilot/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Hosted pilot', path: '/pilot/' }]}
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Hosted pilot</p>
    <h1>12 months of GratisGIS, run for you</h1>
    <p>
      I'm looking for one organization to use GratisGIS for real work. I host it, keep it updated and
      back it up for 12 months, at no cost. You use it, tell me what works and what doesn't, and help
      shape what gets built next.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Applications close {DEADLINE}.</strong></p>

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your own GratisGIS portal at its own address, set up and maintained for you.</li>
      <li>Help moving your first maps and layers in, including from ArcGIS Online.</li>
      <li>A direct line to the person building it, and your requests near the front of the queue.</li>
      <li>
        Your data stays yours. You can export it at any time in standard formats, and at the end you
        can move to your own server with help.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I ask in return</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use it on at least one real workflow, not just a look around.</li>
      <li>A short check-in call once a month.</li>
      <li>Honest feedback, including what's broken or confusing.</li>
      <li>If it goes well, permission to write it up as a case study.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who is a good fit</h2>
    <p>
      Agencies, schools, nonprofits and companies all count. Size matters less than commitment: a team
      of 5 to 50 with a named contact and a real job to do is a better fit than a big name that is only
      exploring. Field data collection, public maps and replacing per-user licenses are all good
      starting points.
    </p>

    <h2>How to apply</h2>
    <p>Answer these questions, either way you prefer:</p>
    <ol>
      {QUESTIONS.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>In public:</strong>{" "}
        <a href={PUBLIC_URL}>post in the Hosted pilot discussion on GitHub</a>. The form asks the same
        questions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Privately:</strong> <a href={mailto}>email {PILOT_EMAIL}</a>. Nothing you send is
        shared.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Before applying, you can try the <a href={DEMO_URL}>public demo</a> to see whether it fits.
    </p>

    <h2>How the pick is made</h2>
    <p>
      I read every application and pick one after {DEADLINE}, based on fit and on who is ready to use it
      for real. Nothing is promised to anyone for applying. Everyone who applies hears back either way.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
